{"kind":"assert","text":"Decision on `f9af044b`: yes, with four conditions.\n\n1. The trace first says, for each failed case, whether the\n   production source or the fixture is wrong. A third copy from a\n   startup recovery is either what section 7 of the note permits or\n   a fault of the client. Say which, with the line. A fixture change\n   never hides a product fault. A product fault gets a product fix\n   and one sentence in the E4 note.\n2. The copy case keeps an exact count. The count is the number that\n   the trace derives, 2 or 3, with its reason in a comment. It keeps\n   the exact assertions of no new version and no new signing.\n3. The expiry case keeps its deadline. The fixture reaches the\n   dispatch gate by a handle that the test owns, with no sleep and\n   no timer.\n4. Rerun the whole `scope-client.test.ts` owner as the baseline of\n   the owner control, once. The other passes carry when only test\n   files change. When production source changes, rerun the compiler\n   contexts and the owners that read that source.\n\nStanding rule from now: a correction of a test that the same\nprivate branch added needs no planner decision when it changes no\nproduction source, removes or loosens no assertion, changes no\ndeadline, and one act states the cause and the correction. Keep the\nfailed attempt. Ask me when the trace finds a product fault that\nchanges a ruling.\n\nCR-2: accepted as you promised it. It rides the version that E4\nopens, because the Page bundle imports the claim code.","body":{"to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}